`testing.B` benchmarks (`topicindex/bench_test.go`) were run natively on arm64, 10 runs
each; values are mean ± 95% CI. N is the cache occupancy and T the number of cached ads
for the requested topic. Full table: §10.1. {{figref:microbench_complexity}} shows each operation against N or T.

{{fig:microbench_complexity}}Cost per call of each `TopicTable` operation against cache occupancy N or topic size T (arm64, 10 runs per point, mean ± s.d.; dashed: linear fits). Registration and lookup grow with the topic's ad count T ({{fit.RegisterFresh.T.small.slope|f:2}} and {{fit.RandomNodes.T.all.slope|f:1}} ns per ad), not with N.{{endfig}}

{{table:micro_fits}}

* **`Register`, fresh ad receiving a wait ticket.** {{fit.RegisterFresh.T.small.intercept|f:0}} ns
  + {{fit.RegisterFresh.T.small.slope|f:2}} ns·T for T ≤ 256, independent of N
  ({{micro.RegisterFresh/N=100/T=16.ns|f:0}} ns at N = 100, {{micro.RegisterFresh/N=10000/T=16.ns|f:0}} ns at N = 10,000).
  The T term is the duplicate check, a linear scan of the topic's linked list. Beyond
  T ≈ 256 the scan leaves the cache and costs {{fit.RegisterFresh.T.all.slope|f:1}} ns per entry
  ({{micro.RegisterFresh/N=5000/T=4096.ns|f:0}} ns at T = 4096). Each call allocates
  {{micro.RegisterFresh/N=5000/T=16.bytes|f:0}} B in {{micro.RegisterFresh/N=5000/T=16.allocs|f:0}} objects.
* **Duplicate `Register`** (the ad is already cached) stops at the duplicate check:
  {{micro.RegisterDuplicate/N=5000/T=1.ns|f:1}} ns at T = 1, {{fit.RegisterDuplicate.T.small.slope|f:2}} ns per entry, no allocations.
* **Admitted retry** (insertion into the lists and the IP tree):
  {{fit.RegisterRetryAdmit.N.all.intercept|f:0}} ns, independent of N.
* **`RandomNodes(topic, 16, check)`** visits every ad of the topic: reservoir sampling
  plus one `netutil.CheckRelayAddr` call per entry, at {{fit.RandomNodes.T.all.slope|f:1}} ns per
  entry. The 16-result limit does not bound the work. An empty topic costs
  {{micro.RandomNodes/N=5000/T=0.ns|f:1}} ns.
* **`Expire`**, in steady state: {{micro.Expire/N=5000/k=1000.ns_per_entry|ci:0}} ns per expired
  entry in batches of 1000, and {{micro.Expire/N=5000/k=1.ns|ci:0}} ns for a single entry. Each
  entry costs {{micro.Expire/N=5000/k=1.allocs|f:0}} allocations.
* **IP score** (tree walk): {{micro.IPScore/shape=diverse/N=10.ns|f:0}}–{{micro.IPScore/shape=diverse/N=100000.ns|f:0}} ns
  for 10–100,000 addresses, logarithmic in N.
* **`RemoveNode`** scans the whole cache: {{fit.RemoveNode.N.all.slope|f:2}} ns·N.

The allocation audit of the simulated baseline (C = 1000, 5 fresh/s, 20 queries/s,
independent ENR copies; 3 seeds) gives the per-call averages under the realistic
operation mix:

{{table:audit}}

Per expired entry this is {{audit.expiry_bytes_per_entry|ci:0}} B in
{{audit.expiry_allocations_per_entry|f:2}} allocations, matching the microbenchmark.

**Latency against occupancy.** At the default capacity C = 1000, with the requested
topic holding a tenth of the cached ads (`BenchmarkOccupancy`, {{figref:latency_vs_occupancy}}):

{{if:figure:latency_vs_occupancy}}{{fig:latency_vs_occupancy}}Registrar-core latency per call against occupancy N/C at C = 1000, with the requested topic holding N/10 ads (arm64, 10 runs, mean ± 95% CI). Wait replies stay at {{occ.register-wait.N100.ns|f:0}}–{{occ.register-wait.N950.ns|f:0}} ns and admissions at {{occ.register-admit.N100.ns|f:0}}–{{occ.register-admit.N950.ns|f:0}} ns; a lookup grows linearly with the topic's ad count to {{occ.lookup.N950.ns|f:0}} ns at 95%.{{endfig}}

{{table:micro_occupancy}}

A wait response costs {{occ.register-wait.N100.ns|f:0}}–{{occ.register-wait.N950.ns|f:0}} ns from 10% to 95%
occupancy and an admission {{occ.register-admit.N100.ns|f:0}}–{{occ.register-admit.N950.ns|f:0}} ns. Both grow
only through the topic size T = N/10 (the duplicate scan); the occupancy factor itself only changes the
computed number. The lookup
grows with the topic size T = N/10: {{occ.lookup.N100.ns|f:0}} ns at 10% and
{{occ.lookup.N950.ns|f:0}} ns at 95%. At N = 0 a registration is always admitted (no wait).
{{else}}*Provisional: the occupancy benchmarks did not run.*{{endif}}

**Validation cost.** `handleRegtopic` does more than the registrar core. Per request it
decodes and verifies the advertiser's record, compares its address with the packet
source, unseals a presented ticket and checks it against the replay memory, and seals
a new ticket for a wait response (`v5_validation_bench_test.go`):

{{if:table:validation}}{{table:validation}}

{{if:share.cgo.total_ns}}For a retry that receives a new ticket, the parts add up as follows (cgo build;
registrar core at 25% occupancy):

{{table:regtopic_share}}

The registrar core is {{share.cgo.core_fraction|pct:1}} of this per-request work; ENR signature
verification is {{share.cgo.enr_fraction|pct:0}}. Without cgo (pure-Go secp256k1) the core's
share drops to {{share.purego.core_fraction|pct:1}}. Packet decryption, session handling
and response encoding come on top and are not measured.{{endif}}
{{else}}*Provisional: the validation benchmarks did not run.*{{endif}}
